    bucketing is testable without faking clocks.
  - [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[0m — wires it together and renders.

  The reason attention is a pure derivation and not a stored thing is the whole point of Approach A: there is no
  notification state that can disagree with the tasks, because there is no notification state.

  [1m3. UI[0m

  Single page. An attention panel at the top of [38;5;153m<main>[39m showing what needs attention, grouped as Overdue / Today / Soon
  (next 3 days), each row with a [1mSnooze 1 day[0m button. Below it, the task list: add form, checkbox, title, due date,
  delete.

  The panel hides itself entirely when nothing needs attention — an empty "0 notifications" element is noise.

  Re-render happens on any state change, plus a 60-second interval so the panel updates when the clock crosses midnight
  without a reload.

  [1m4. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m can fail — disabled in the browser, quota exceeded, or holding corrupt JSON from an earlier version. On
  load, bad data gets validated and dropped per-record rather than taking down the whole list, and the app falls back to
  in-memory state with a non-blocking notice. A task app that shows a blank screen because one stored record is
  malformed is worse than one that loses that record.

  [1m5. Testing[0m

  The logic worth testing is all in the pure modules: attention bucketing (boundary cases around today/overdue, snooze
  expiry, completed tasks), task operations, and storage round-tripping including the malformed-input path. Rendering
  gets light coverage at most.

  Since this is a fresh repo with no tooling configured, that's a decision to make now — it's cheapest before any code
  exists:

